<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Adolf Frederick, King of Sweden</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1710">1710</a>–<a href="/years/1771">1771</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Adolf Frederick, King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>, was a prince of the house of Holstein-Gottorp who served as prince-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lubeck">Lübeck</a> from 1727 to 1750. Chosen heir to the Swedish throne in 1743 under Russian pressure, he became king in 1751. His reign fell in Sweden's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/age-of-liberty">Age of Liberty</a>, when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/riksdag">Riksdag</a> held real power, and his attempts to strengthen the crown failed.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/adolf-frederick-king-of-sweden.jpg" alt="Adolf Frederick, King of Sweden" width="320" height="412" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gustaf Lundberg · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gustaf_Lundberg_-_Portrait_of_Adolf_Frederick,_King_of_Sweden_-_WGA13779.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Adolf Frederick was born on 14 May 1710 at Gottorf Castle, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schleswig">Schleswig</a>, a member of a junior line of the House of Holstein-Gottorp. His father, Christian August, had been Protestant Prince-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lubeck">Lübeck</a> since 1705 and acted during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-northern-war">Great Northern War</a> as administrator of Holstein-Gottorp for his underage nephew, Duke Charles Frederick. His mother, Albertina Frederica of Baden-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durlach">Durlach</a>, descended from earlier Swedish royalty.</p><p>From 1727 to 1750 he was prince-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lubeck">Lübeck</a>, ruling a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fief</a> around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eutin">Eutin</a>. After Charles Frederick died in 1739 he became administrator of Holstein-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kiel">Kiel</a> for the duke's orphan son, Charles Peter Ulrich, who was soon invited to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> by his aunt, Empress Elizabeth, and was later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-iii-of-russia">Peter III of Russia</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>In 1743 the Hat faction elected Adolf Frederick heir to the throne of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>, wanting better conditions from Empress Elizabeth at the Treaty of Åbo. In 1744 he married Louisa Ulrika of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>, a sister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-the-great">Frederick the Great</a>. On the death of Frederick I on 25 March 1751 he became the first Swedish king from the House of Holstein-Gottorp, and he was crowned in Stockholm Cathedral on 26 November 1751.</p><p>Real power lay with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/riksdag-of-the-estates">Riksdag of the Estates</a>, often distracted by party strife. Twice he tried to free himself from its control. In 1756, urged on by Louisa Ulrika, he took part in a coup to abolish the rule of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/riksdag">Riksdag</a> and restore <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/absolute-monarchy">absolute monarchy</a>, and nearly lost his throne. In the December Crisis of 1768, guided by his eldest son Gustav, he overthrew the Cap senate but could make no use of the victory.</p><p>The reign saw a long period of internal peace, but finances stagnated under the mercantilist policies of the Hat administration. In the parliament of 1765–1766 the opposition took over the government and enacted reforms towards economic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liberalism">liberalism</a>, as well as a Freedom of Press Act that ended censorship, keeping punishment only for libelling the monarch or the Church of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Adolf Frederick died suddenly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stockholm">Stockholm</a> on 12 February 1771, with symptoms resembling heart failure or poisoning. Stories that he died of an enormous meal are considered propaganda by modern writers. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustav-iii">Gustav III</a> seized power in a military coup in 1772 and restored absolute rule. The king was regarded as dependent on others and lacking the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/talent">talents</a> of a statesman, but was reported to be a good husband and a gentle master. He had a talent for astronomy, music and military matters, and his favourite pastime was making snuffboxes. He and Louisa Ulrika had five children, among them Gustav III and Charles XIII.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Adolf Frederick of Sweden” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>One of them, <b>Adolf Frederick</b>, became King of Sweden in 1751.</blockquote><a href="/state/prince-bishopric-of-lubeck">Prince-Bishopric of Lübeck</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Gottorf Castle, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in The Royal Court Parish, in what is now <a href="/countries/sweden">Sweden</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=54.51,9.54&amp;year=1749" aria-label="Gottorf Castle on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4760 297 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Adolf Frederick, King of Sweden"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5210 567h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5210 567h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=54.51,9.54&amp;year=1749">What was here in 1749? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
